{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>When you lift up the head, etc.</b> One needs to know the reason for His saying <b>תשא</b> and not saying תפקוד as He said regarding the tribe of Levi (Numbers 3:15). Second, His saying <b>את ראש</b> and not being satisfied to say ״כי תשא את בני ישראל״ — for one should not say that HaShem commanded counting only the leaders, since the matter itself contradicts this. Third, one needs to know the intent of His saying <b>לפקדיהם</b>." "And the verse is explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory, for the sinner causes, through his sin, a bending of his head, for the nature of evil is that its essence looks toward the earth, for it is lowly, whereas the nature of holiness is the lifting of the head and the elevation of essence and quality. And you will find that Scripture said (Genesis 23:17) ״ויקם שדה עפרון״, and the Sages of blessed memory expounded (Genesis Rabbah 58:8): it had a standing, an elevation. Now, since this commandment came after the incident of the calf, as the verse precisely says in its wording <b>ונתת אתו על עבודת אהל מועד</b>, and the Sages of blessed memory said (Rashi): from here we learn that they were commanded to count them after the incident of the calf — this is what it means by <b>כי תשא</b>, in the language of lifting the head, in the manner of (Genesis 40:13) ״ישא פרעה את ראשך״, which is an expression of raising — that you shall lift the head of the children of Israel, for their head was lowered because of their deficiency in the incident of the calf. And this is His saying <b>לפקדיהם</b>, in the manner of (Numbers 31:49) ״ולא נפקד ממנו איש״, and the Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 64a) that not a man was found among them who had sinned. <b>ונתנו איש כפר נפשו לה׳</b> — who had sinned against the soul. <b>בפקד אתם</b> — meaning as it sounds." "And the reason for His saying <b>ונתנו</b> with the addition of the vav is because there must first be recognition of the sin, for what he did was not good and he sinned against the soul, and afterward he shall give the ransom of his soul to HaShem." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by His saying <b>לפקדיהם</b> etc., that you have no permission to count them except for the cause of their being counted — meaning, on account of their deficiency, if some were missing from them and you wish to ascertain the remaining ones; or because they entered the category of supplying what is lacking, as He said (Numbers 31:49) ״עבדיך נשאו את ראש״ etc., because they went down to battle; or because of the need for the number to array battle against an enemy and the like — and it comes to exclude, that one may not count them without any cause at all. And His saying <b>ונתנו</b> with the addition of the vav is to make this an additional condition upon the first condition: that not only do they need to be for their counting, but also each man must give a ransom, etc. And this matter applies only to that time when Israel were liable on account of the calf, but for future generations there is no need for the giving. And see the verse that follows this one." "<b>And there shall be no plague among them when you count them.</b> One needs to know why He repeated saying a second time <b>בפקד אתם</b>. And it appears that since the commandment came at a time when they were liable to Heaven on account of the sin of the calf, one might say that at another time, when one wishes to count, there is no strictness in the matter and he may count them one by one. Therefore He said <b>ולא יהיה בהם נגף בפקד אתם</b> — He included any other count besides the first, for upon it too the warning comes, that if he counts them there will be a plague among them." "It further intends along this line: that besides the fact that through the giving they make atonement for their souls, there is further ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה בָהֶם נֶגֶף״ on account of counting them — and from the conclusion of the matter you know that the counting, of itself, brings about the plague." "Further, it may intend that HaShem commanded two things: the first, that the counting be by means of giving something, as He said <b>וְנָתְנוּ</b> etc., and as He explained in saying <b>זֶה יִתְּנוּ</b> — and the reason is for the atonement of their souls. And the second, that they not be counted one by one but by means of something else — and that is His saying <b>וְלֹא יִהְיֶה בָהֶם נֶגֶף בִּפְקֹד אֹתָם</b> one by one, but by means of something else. And this manner of counting does not produce any benefit but only averts the harm that flows from the counting itself. And it is what we find with Saul, of whom it is written (I Samuel 11:8) ״וַיִּפְקְדֵם בְּבָזֶק״ — meaning, with sherds. And without our explanation it is difficult: who permitted Saul to count Israel with sherds, when the Torah said ״זֶה יִתְּנוּ כָּל הָעוֹבֵר עַל הַפְּקוּדִים מַחֲצִית הַשֶּׁקֶל״? But according to our words you will find satisfaction in the matter." "And I set my heart to understand the matter of David (II Samuel 24), for according to what is heard from the verses he counted Israel one by one without an intermediary — in addition to counting them for nothing, and these are astonishing things, that King David, upon him be peace, should do so strange a thing. And we find that our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 62b) said that from Heaven they caused him to stumble in a matter that even schoolchildren know, etc.; for the reason of His saying (I Samuel 26:19) ״אִם ה׳ הֱסִיתְךָ בִּי״ — their words may be examined there. The meaning of their words: because he called HaShem, perish the thought, a misleader — a created evil angel was called forth, according to what they said earlier (Avot 4:11) ״הָעוֹבֵר עֲבֵרָה אַחַת קוֹנֶה לוֹ קָטֵיגוֹר אֶחָד,״ and it is the very species of the sin, like its own deed; and therefore an evil angel, a misleader, was created from it, and it incited him and misled him into doing so, in the secret of their saying (ibid.) ״עֲבֵרָה גּוֹרֶרֶת עֲבֵרָה.״ And you will find that the sin made its own image: just as David's words in saying ״הֱסִיתְךָ״ were inadvertent on his part — and perish the thought that he intended to speak improper words regarding the Supernal, and who is a God-fearing servant like David — but it was inadvertent, so too he was incited by the inciter to do something unfitting, as though intentionally." "Yet it remains difficult. We can grant regarding David that we say he erred, but what can we answer concerning Joab? Either way it is difficult: if David's words came to him simply as ״פְּקוֹד בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ who told him that David's intention was not to tell him to count them according to the Torah — and from the plain sense there would be no need to tell him something that schoolchildren know. And if we say that David explicitly commanded him to count them one by one and not by means of a half-shekel, it is difficult how he accepted his words when he said so, for he should have refused, since ״דִּבְרֵי הָרַב וְדִבְרֵי הַתַּלְמִיד דִּבְרֵי מִי שׁוֹמְעִין״ — and this is so even regarding a royal commandment, and this is a broad halakhah (Rambam, Laws of Kings, ch. 3). And even if Joav were hidden from him and a single incident happened to David and Joav, what can we answer concerning the Sanhedrin? What can we answer concerning every man when they saw that they came to count them? They should have trembled with a great trembling and terrors of death fallen upon them, and cried out about the matter before Joav and before the king. Moreover, they could have come with a heard argument: ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה בָהֶם נֶגֶף בִּפְקֹד אֹתָם.״ And if they were more concerned about a half-shekel than about their own bodies, they should have demanded that they be counted with sherds like the deed of King Saul — and how did all the sons of a living El not have pity on their own souls?" "<b>And what is correct</b> in my eyes is that David said his words to Joav in general terms, and the matter is simple — that Joav knew a book and did not count them except by means of something else, with bezek or the like. And you will find that the Talmud was precise in its language (Berakhot 62b): ״לֹא שָׁקַל מִנַּיְיהוּ כּוֹפֶר״ — this implies: a ransom is what he did not take from them, but the counting was by means of something else. And so we have deduced that not every counting requires a giving, and the error he committed was that the counting was for nothing — and we have already explained from the fine point of the verse that they were not to be counted except for the sake of a thing, as we have written above, but for nothing, no. And this is the argument that you will find Joav raised to David, that there is no need for the matter to count Israel. And perhaps it is that if he had counted them by means of a half-shekel — even if Israel would thereby be liable to death — behold it is a ransom for the soul of each one. And that is what our Sages of blessed memory said: that if David had counted them by means of the shekels there would have been no plague among them. And you will find that the verse says (II Samuel 24:1) ״וַיּוֹסֶף ה׳ לַחֲרוֹת בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל וַיָּסֶת אֶת דָּוִד בָּהֶם״ — see, this lets you know that Israel were already liable for what had come before, and therefore when the counting came upon them for no purpose, with no authorization from the Master of all, the attribute of judgment held sway over them and exacted punishment from them for their sin." "<b>Further</b>, it is possible to say that since a condition of the census was mentioned — that it must be for a cause — whoever lacks even one condition is included in the category of those subject to plague aside from counting. And this was the error of David, who erred. Therefore you find in Joab's reply to him: ״וַאדֹנִי הַמֶּלֶךְ לָמָּה חָפֵץ בַּדָּבָר הַזֶּה״ (II Samuel 24:3) — meaning, a census for no need; but if there was a need in the matter, he too would agree. And if the census had been one by one, even if there had been a need for the census, it would not have been counted in that manner, and he would have needed to say to him how he could do such a thing. But since this matter is not explicit in the verse but only derived by inference, Joab found no grounds to dispute with David. Or it may be that when he saw that the king's word was insistent upon him, he said perhaps he has a hidden reason, and he was wary of rebelling against the kingdom; but he never counted them one by one. And by this there is also no difficulty regarding every man of Israel who did not protect himself, for they were not counted one by one, and this matter — that one must count them by means of a cause — is not incumbent upon them to know whether the king has a cause, and they did not investigate behind it to verify its propriety." "We find ourselves saying the general principle: the number of the children of Israel is divided into three categories. The first is the count that took place after the calf — this was for a need, and it was also necessary to give a half-shekel, because they were obligated as mentioned. Second, whenever there is a need for their number, there is no need to count them by means of shekels but even with potsherds, and this was the deed of Saul. Third, whenever there is no need to count them, they may not be counted even with potsherds, and this was the error of David, which HaShem induced upon him because of his error; and by giving the half-shekel it is permitted, for this prevents the plague that comes from the count. And if you say, why did David need a count, and was the tally of the shekels of the children of Israel brought year by year not sufficient for him? — it may be answered that there is no proof from the shekels, for Joab brought only the number of men who draw the sword (II Samuel 24:9), whereas the shekels include everyone, even the minors, as they said (Shekalim 1:3): a person may weigh the half-shekel on behalf of his minor son and daughter, and on behalf of his wife, etc. Therefore he wished to know the number of men of war, and this matter he could not know from the half-shekel brought to the House of HaShem."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :register {:flags {7 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:29.784421343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}